<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Khwarizmi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 780–c. 850</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Khwarizmi was a Persian mathematician, astronomer and geographer who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> in the ninth century. His treatise on calculation by completion and balancing gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a> its name, and the word algorithm derives from his name. His geographical work also recorded information about lands far beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, which is why he is cited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>'s profile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-khwarizmi.jpg" alt="al-Khwarizmi" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zarateman · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madrid_-_Ciudad_Universitaria,_Monumento_a_Muhammad_al-Juarismi_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Few details of al-Khwarizmi's life are known with certainty. Ibn al-Nadim gives his birthplace as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, a region of Greater Iran now divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, and he is generally thought to have come from there; his name means from Khwarazm. He was of Persian descent, probably Khwarezmian, but wrote his scientific works entirely in Arabic. The historian al-Tabari gives him the further epithets al-Majusi and al-Qutrubbulli. Roshdi Rashed holds that these belong to a second person, whereas David A. King affirms that he was born just outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, at Qutrubbul. The epithet al-Majusi may point to Zoroastrian forebears, but the pious preface to his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a> shows that he was an orthodox Muslim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Most of al-Khwarizmi's work was done between 813 and 833. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> had become the centre of scientific studies and trade, and around 820 he was appointed astronomer and head of the library of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wisdom">House of Wisdom</a>, which the Abbasid caliph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> had established. He studied the sciences and mathematics, including the translation of Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> scientific <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. He is said to have assisted a project to determine the circumference of the Earth and to have overseen 70 geographers in making a world map for al-Ma'mun. During the reign of al-Wathiq he is said to have taken part in the first of two embassies to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His treatise on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">algebra</a>, compiled between 813 and 833, presented the first systematic solution of linear and quadratic equations by reducing them to six standard forms using the operations al-jabr, restoration, and al-muqabala, balancing, and it gave algebra its name. It was written with the encouragement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mamun">al-Ma'mun</a> and is full of examples from trade, surveying and legal inheritance. Robert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> translated it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> in 1145, and it was used until the 16th century as the principal mathematical textbook of European universities. His book on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> numerals, written about 825, spread the Hindu-Arabic numeral system, and its Latin translation in the 12th century introduced the term algorithm, from the Latinized form of his name, to the Western world.</p><p>His Zij as-Sindhind, based on Indian astronomical methods, held 116 tables for the Sun, Moon and five planets, and marked a turning point in Islamic astronomy. His Geography, finished in 833, reworked Ptolemy's and listed 2,402 coordinates; it corrected Ptolemy's overestimate of the length of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, 63 degrees of longitude, to nearly 50. He also wrote on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrolabe">astrolabe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundial">sundial</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> calendar, and produced a book of annals that does not survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Khwarizmi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The word 'algebra' comes from al-jabr in the title of <b>al-Khwarizmi</b>'s treatise on calculation, written in Abbasid Baghdad around 820.</blockquote><a href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…of the Abbasid caliphate and after, scholars working in Arabic preserved and translated Greek philosophy and science and made advances of their own in mathematics, with <b>al-Khwarizmi</b>’s algebra; in astronomy, optics and medicine, with Ibn al-Haytham and Ibn Sina; and in history and social thought, with Ibn Khaldun.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>English words such as algebra, alcohol, sugar, coffee, cotton and zero come from Arabic, and algorithm comes from the name of the 9th-century Baghdad mathematician <b>al-Khwarizmi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/arabic">Arabic · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The mathematician <b>al-Khwarizmi</b>, working in Baghdad in the early ninth century, wrote a treatise explaining the Indian decimal numerals and another on the methods of solving equations, whose title gave Europe the word “algebra”.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=825" aria-label="Baghdad on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5684 954 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of al-Khwarizmi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6134 1224h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,44.37&amp;year=825">What was here in 825 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…has encouraged me to compose a short work on Calculating by (the rules of) Completion and Reduction, confining it to what is easiest and most useful in arithmetic, such as men constantly require in cases of inheritance, legacies, partition, law-suits, and trade…&#8221;</p><footer>The Algebra, author's preface &#183; tr. Frederic Rosen, 1831 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Compendious_Book_on_Calculation_by_Completion_and_Balancing/The_author%E2%80%99s_preface" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The learned in times which have passed away, and among nations which have ceased to exist, were constantly employed in writing books on the several departments of science and on the various branches of knowledge…&#8221;</p><footer>The Algebra, author's preface &#183; tr. Frederic Rosen, 1831 &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Compendious_Book_on_Calculation_by_Completion_and_Balancing/The_author%E2%80%99s_preface" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
